closure_check (c2-t2, 2026-11-04, version 02): the final closure check of c2-at's SOURCE013_CHAIN_CONDITIONS_v01,
items 8 and 9, as a conditional program (c2-coord 07:08Z item 3(a)). Text: notes/T2_CLOSURE_CHECK_v02.md.
It binds c2-t1's near part NEAR_CHECK_v02 (c2-t1 note 87; copied unchanged into nearcopy_v02/, hashes pinned in the
inputs), uses its printed tuple, and recomputes its two near quantities independently as a transcription check.
Interface (after AT2307 to AT2309, which apply to version 01 of this program as well): every value that enters a
decision is an exact rational. e_up and C_N are the printed uppers of NEAR_CHECK_v02; C0 = C_rest + C_N; D_out is a
decimal certified <= the arb ball of D; margins are exact and displayed rounded down; room = D_out - C0 rounded down;
C0/D_out rounded up; the beta_* needed is exact and displayed rounded up. Arb balls are used only inside the CE cell
and the transcription checks.
Every input is a named parameter of inputs_v02.json with a sense (upper, lower, exact, choice) and a status. Arithmetic
is exact (Fraction) or outward arb (python-flint, 200 bits). Each check is reported as
  PASS       it holds, and every input it uses is CONFIRMED (or a choice);
  PASS-COND  it holds at the given values, but some input is a stand-in (STANDIN-FLOAT or STANDIN-TARGET),
             so the check proves nothing;
  FAIL       it does not hold at the given values (a failed sufficient test; it excludes nothing);
  OPEN       a premise that no computation here supplies.
The closure statement (block I) follows only if, for one case, every check of the accepted H4 reading is PASS and
every named hypothesis of block H is supplied by an accepted certificate. The CE part (block G) is c2-t1's
budget_013h code (ps1copy/), used unchanged.
Usage: python3 -B closure_check_v02.py [inputs.json]   (writes closure_check_v02.json next to this file)
